Given, expansion is $\left[\sqrt{\left(\frac{x}{3}\right)}+\sqrt{\left(\frac{3}{2 x^{2}}\right)}\right]^{10}$
Let $t_{r+1}$ be general term, then

\begin{aligned}
t_{r+1} &=\binom{10}{r}\left(\sqrt{\frac{x}{3}}\right)^{10-r}\left(\sqrt{\frac{3}{2 x^{2}}}\right)^{r} \\
&=\binom{10}{r} \frac{x \frac{10-r}{2}}{\frac{10-r}{2}} \cdot \frac{3^{i / 2}}{2^{r / 2} \cdot x^{r}}=\binom{10}{r} x-\frac{r}{2}-r \cdot \frac{3^{r-5}}{2^{r / 2}}
\end{aligned}

For the term independent of $x$.
Puts $5-\frac{r}{2}-r=0$
\Rightarrow \quad 5-\frac{3}{2} r=0 \Rightarrow \frac{3}{2} r=5 \Rightarrow r=\frac{10}{3}

Fractional value of $r$ is not possible. So, no term is independent of $x$.

The middle term in the expansion of $(1+x)^{2 n}$ is $\left(\frac{2 n}{2}+1\right)$ th term i.e. $(n+1)$ th term.

\begin{aligned}
&=\frac{(2 n) !}{n ! n !} x^{n} \\
&=\frac{2 n(2 n-1)(2 n-2) \ldots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1}{\{n(n-1)(n-2) \ldots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1\}^{2}} x^{n} \\
&=\frac{(2 n(2 n-2)(2 n-4) \ldots 4.2\}\{(2 n-1)(2 n-3) \ldots(3 \cdot 1)\}}{\{n(n-1)(n-2) \ldots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1\}^{2}} x^{n} \\
&=\frac{2^{n}\{n(n-1)(n-2) \ldots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1\}\{((2 n-1)(2 n-3) \ldots 3 \cdot 1\}}{\{n(n-1)(n-2) \ldots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1\}^{2}} x^{n} \\
&=\frac{2^{n}\{(2 n-1)(2 n-3) \ldots 3 \cdot 1\}}{\{n(n-1)(n-2) \ldots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1\}} x^{n} \\
&=\frac{(2 n-1)(2 n-3) \ldots 3 \cdot 1}{n !} x^{n} 2^{n}
\end{aligned}
